% cat arg.sh
#!/bin/sh

echo " argc = $#."
echo " \$1 = >$1<."

p_a()
{
echo " p_a.  argc = $#."
echo " p_a.  \$1 = >$1<."
}

p_a "$@"


Now, on Solaris (SunOS 4.1.4 or 5.7):

% ./arg.sh
 argc = 0.
 $1 = ><.
 p_a.  argc = 0.
 p_a.  $1 = ><.

While on Tru64 (5.1A):

% uname -a
OSF1 pxp.provis.com V5.1 1885 alpha

% ./arg.sh
 argc = 0.         <-----+
 $1 = ><.                |
 p_a.  argc = 1.   <-----+
 p_a.  $1 = ><.


   Add an argument, and all the zeros turn to ones, as expected.  This
quirk upsets some scripts I'd prefer not to have to change.  Any wisdom?

